00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:12.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i want to share with you all a recent  excursion of mine exploring pop music  now during the last five or six years or  so i've been exploring different genres  and i've been compiling this list of  different genres that i've been meaning  to cover and honestly pop was not on  this list but what happened was i took  on this game where i was pretty much  forced to do a mini pop study so  basically the job was to record 10 made  up piano intros that were inspired by a  list of popular songs from different  generations and at first i didn't think  much about it but the project turned  into a very interesting subject for me  this was one of the first songs that i  listened to and the things that stood  out to me first was the register  pretty close together very efficiently  voiced as opposed to something wider  also the way it's played it has this  sort of  straightforward quality  uh not just the tempo but pianistically  i feel almost feel like there are these  micro slaps  happening because you really can't play  it too  loose or loosely  it doesn't sound as right i think  also of course the syncopation  if i were to change a few things here or  maybe just take the first few notes  maybe the first  four  i could probably turn it into  [Music]  something like this which is classical  and not pop sounding at all  or  if i were to  [Music]  let me okay  something like that maybe also  i'm using those first four notes  but as soon as you go back down  this progression is  not commonly found in classical music so  [Applause]  anyway  that is one for example  i took all those things and try to  intuitively come up with a distant  cousin version of it which ended up  sounding like this  [Applause]  if i were to spread the hands apart  it doesn't sound as poppy  or  have everything down here  maybe get rid of the syncopations  also less poppy  this is another one that i listen to  of course i said what i did about  the lower register but here of course  it sits perfectly fine in this lower  register i think it's because of the  increased simplicity of  of the pattern  if i were to just keep one direction  it almost sounds like  moonlight  it's also very consistent  it's just going up and down the cord  and i was going for that loosely  speaking in my version which was maybe  less of a  further cousin from this  than the other one was to a thousand  miles  [Music]  all right so you get the point  afterwards i continued to just ponder  about this it became interesting to me  and  it just sparked something in me to  continue to ask the question what  exactly is pop music what makes pop  music pop music the first thing that i  did was actually call my good friend  andrew huang who's helped me with so  many different things because he is a  master at so many different genres and  so many different skill sets so i called  him up and i asked him this very  question you're in a unique position  where  you do so many genres sometimes when i  see your  instagram posts or your youtube videos  you would specifically identify this is  more of my pop side or this is more of  my experimental side so what are you  tapping into when you  say that this is more of a pop side of  me well for me i specify things because  i've just found over time it helps to  contextualize  things for the listener helps to set  expectations  and when you're outputting a lot of  different kinds of things  i just feel like that is a helpful thing  to do rather than people being  completely shocked or something and when  i say pop i think that um i mean it's a  term that means that has meant a lot of  different things over the decades but i  think essentially it's like this idea of  creating a song in such a way that it's  meant to be fun  catchy  singable have a bit more um immediate  appeal  immediately a lot i like that yeah yeah  yeah i think it's it yeah it's about  being accessible i guess okay but that  changes of course over every several  years we have a completely new sound of  pop is there a way to define it as a  genre  musically  i think that um it  tends to have a very similar structure  very kind of like predictable  three choruses  almost everything is based around the  basic kind of verse chorus verse chorus  bridge chorus form even though there may  be variations within that i think  basically from the point where we were  recording singers  probably vocals have been a prominent  feature of like there's very little  instrumental pop music but there's  periods of time where i feel like  some  sounds of pop  you couldn't really define us other  things i don't maybe maybe that's not so  i'm thinking about like the 90s  [Music]  a boy band you know backstreet boys  right  that that just feels like pure pop to me  even though i guess you know there's  maybe they're probably borrowing from  like underground dance music of the time  and certainly the songwriting might have  had a history throughout like  you know somewhat older styles but um  yeah there are certain sounds that feel  like they are just  pop in a way i think well i guess this  is true for many genres you don't  necessarily have to have the whole  checklist of elements to be able to  make it you know to be able to apply the  labels so i think  plenty uh like actually any pop song if  you performed it just on the piano i  feel like still retains just enough of  the  poppiness to it kind of i think  exploring that line  of like where does it become pop that is  usually when i do a genre study like  this i go to my instagram and i ask the  many wonderful individuals that follow  me over there what their favorite song  in a particular genre was and just  cycling through all the answers i found  one that was very piano centric that i  thought was perfect for doing some  further experimentation with it's  somebody you loved by lewis capaldi  and when i was at the piano i decided  that i would take the beginning portion  of the song at least and cycle it  through different genres and different  styles  [Music]  to the best of my ability of course i'm  more familiar with some than i am with  others  [Music]  i just had some fun trying to find that  point where  it becomes more or less pop and to my  surprise this was a lot harder than i  thought it would be because  it was not a matter of just shifting a  few things  first in terms of harmony there are four  chords that are repeated throughout the  song  if this sounds a little familiar i think  it's because it's only one chord away  from being packable's canon  here i just changed the rhythm slightly  while keeping the melody the same  [Music]  and when i changed the chords a bit and  still kept the melody it sounded like it  could have still been a part of the  original  then i changed the chords further and  added the sort of bossanova feel  once i changed certain things about the  melody it was no longer the song  and for certain genres i needed to  change  so much more than just a few tweaks here  and there  for example here where i was trying to  imitate the style of chopin i really had  to change the chord progression quite a  bit and was only able to retain a small  portion of the melody  when i'm doing these genre studies i'm  really driven simply by curiosity as a  listener a player  a music appreciator in general but also  there's a part of me as a composer that  is very interested in trying out  different things yeah even if it doesn't  stick to what i normally do just in  practice try something out experiment  with it and in this case i set out to  write a popish piece not necessarily a  pop song or a fully pop piece  i guess you all will let me know how pop  i got  but i  was paying attention to a few things  first  the bridge chorus bridge chorus  oh no the verse chorus verse chorus  bridge  chorus  form  um yes anyway to me that form is sort of  like going between  two areas like a teeter-totter and then  going to a different space and then  coming back to this teeter-totter i  don't know if that made any sense but to  me that's how i thought of it  also i  really tried to come up with a catchy or  memorable melody  [Music]  that's part one  part two  and i was thinking of the chords that go  underneath it and when i usually do that  i tend to come up with these patterns  that i favor a lot of times when i'm  improvising or writing music here are  the three chords for my piece  that's really detached and chaotic i  don't know i just like that kind of  sound  so this was prior to recording the  actual arrangement i was just playing  around with the chords and this might  have been the default direction that i  would have taken  and as soon as i started doing that it  lost all of its catchiness or  memorable qualities so i i really  changed directions and decided that i  was going to keep it simple  and basically i just kept looping that  chord progression  [Music]  after the bridge and then coming back to  the original with again a few tweaks  i  ended the piece with a sort of fade out  i thought that was appropriate  and  what else yeah i think that's it  here's the piece  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  this is so familiar and so obvious to me  that i almost feel like  this melody has already been written and  if it if it has been i'm so sorry i  didn't mean to plagiarize or  copy at all  i just this was my best attempt at  looking for something catchy  so if it already exists it's probably  just subconscious regurgitation but  anyway i hope you found this interesting  for me it was especially intriguing as a  composer because  just the practice of  coming up with a melody that is very  distinct like this  i thought was great  a great exercise and  yeah having a melody is  quite cool it's something to think about  something to chew on and other than that  let me know your thoughts in the  comments and i will see you very soon in  the next video bye  [Music]

